= Disease Course and Prognosis
<ch:disease-course>

#chapter-abstract[
ME/CFS is not a static condition: its course varies widely across patients and over time, from gradual or sudden onset to episodic fluctuation, and from mild to very severe presentations. This chapter characterizes the disease course in detail — the patterns of onset, the severity spectrum and its functional correlates, the typical trajectories of improvement or deterioration, and the factors that shape long-term prognosis. It then examines patient subgroups and the reality of severe and very severe disease, in which individuals may be bedbound or housebound, and closes by considering the triggers and mechanisms that can initiate or perpetuate the illness. Understanding this variability is essential for prognosis counselling, for severity-appropriate care, and for designing trials that account for the heterogeneous natural history of the disease.
]

#roadmap(title: [How to Use This Chapter])[
*For patients*: read the onset-patterns (@sec:onset) and severity-levels (@sec:severity) sections to understand your own course, the progression section (@sec:progression) for what may lie ahead, and the prognosis section (@sec:prognosis) for realistic expectations. If you are severe, the severe-reality section (@sec:severe-reality) speaks directly to your experience.

*For caregivers*: read the severity-levels (@sec:severity) and progression (@sec:progression) sections to understand fluctuating disability, and the severe-reality section (@sec:severe-reality) if you support a severe patient.

*For clinicians*: read the onset-patterns (@sec:onset), severity-levels (@sec:severity), and prognosis (@sec:prognosis) sections to inform prognosis counselling and severity-appropriate care, and the subgroups section (@sec:subgroups) to recognize patient subtypes.

*For researchers*: read the subgroups (@sec:subgroups) and prognosis (@sec:prognosis) sections to understand the heterogeneity that affects trial design and participant selection, and the onset-patterns and trigger-mechanisms sections (@sec:onset, @sec:trigger-mechanisms-overview) for disease-initiation hypotheses.
]

